WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:22.420 --> 00:00:54.140
[Music]

00:00:54.320 --> 00:00:58.680
[Narrator:] You're about to see a motion picture, which is going to help you to understand more clearly

00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:02.646
some of the physical and emotional symptoms that have been bothering you,

00:01:02.646 --> 00:01:06.140
and which are responsible for you being here as patients in this hospital.

00:01:07.560 --> 00:01:11.820
To explain the causes of your symptoms to you, we are going to show you,

00:01:11.820 --> 00:01:17.913
among other things, some combat scenes, scenes just like those some of you have been in.

00:01:18.820 --> 00:01:25.313
Now, they may upset you, but just remember that no one ever had an operation or an illness or had a broken leg fixed

00:01:25.320 --> 00:01:27.480
without experiencing a little pain.

00:01:28.220 --> 00:01:30.020
It's the same way with feelings.

00:01:30.940 --> 00:01:37.720
When we dislodge sore feelings, we have to pry them loose like a bad tooth, and that hurts.

00:01:38.040 --> 00:01:39.700
Expect it to hurt a little.

00:01:39.960 --> 00:01:43.680
And strangely enough, you will find it doesn't hurt half as much as you anticipate.

00:01:44.920 --> 00:01:46.340
So now let's get started.

00:01:47.420 --> 00:01:49.740
The natural place to start is with fear.

00:01:50.940 --> 00:01:53.120
Everybody is afraid some of the time.

00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:59.560
And everybody going into battle is always afraid, at least if they're normal like us.

00:02:00.620 --> 00:02:04.880
And that's a good thing, for fear has its uses.

00:02:05.880 --> 00:02:10.800
It makes us alert, sends the blood through our veins, gives us strength and sharpness.

00:02:12.020 --> 00:02:13.420
Let's take a simple example.

00:02:14.220 --> 00:02:18.200
Here is a cat behaving in a perfectly normal manner eating its supper.

00:02:19.460 --> 00:02:22.600
But what happens when her natural enemy, the dog, walks by?

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:25.880
She immediately gets into condition red.

00:02:26.540 --> 00:02:28.940
And condition red means that an attack is imminent.

00:02:29.440 --> 00:02:31.040
So she's on her guard.

00:02:31.320 --> 00:02:33.740
Yet being on guard looks like fear.

00:02:34.280 --> 00:02:35.820
And that's the clue.

00:02:36.580 --> 00:02:38.340
Being on guard is fear.

00:02:39.520 --> 00:02:42.340
Fear is the body's way of getting into condition red.

00:02:42.840 --> 00:02:48.080
She is, as you were, at your battle stations and in your foxholes, all set to go.

00:02:49.260 --> 00:02:52.620
That's nature's logical answer to danger, fear.

00:02:54.360 --> 00:03:01.280
Fear keys you up, sets your heart pounding, gives you that tingling feeling in your legs and up and down your spine.

00:03:02.360 --> 00:03:06.840
It makes your mouth dry and causes the all-gone feeling in the pit of your stomach.

00:03:07.460 --> 00:03:11.080
You get restless, you're on your mark, set to go.

00:03:11.760 --> 00:03:15.920
[ Artillery shells exploding ]

00:03:16.080 --> 00:03:17.760
[Soldier:] Come on, get the lead out.

00:03:22.520 --> 00:03:24.480
[Narrator:] But you know all about this.

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:26.700
Let's go back to our cat. She can teach us a lot.

00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:32.720
When the dog, her enemy, goes, fear goes with him, and everything comes back to normal.

00:03:33.460 --> 00:03:38.620
Once again, she's a peaceful tabby enjoying her supper, quite as if nothing had happened.

00:03:39.500 --> 00:03:46.560
Once our danger has passed, you and I react in the same way, go back to our normal occupations, with one difference.

00:03:46.780 --> 00:03:48.580
We remember.

00:03:49.260 --> 00:03:56.013
And sometimes this remembering business is so real, it won't let us relax. It keeps us on our guard,

00:03:56.020 --> 00:03:58.147
even though there's nothing happening to make us afraid.

00:03:59.560 --> 00:04:04.380
For instance, here's a hospital ward, probably pretty much like the one you're in.

00:04:05.080 --> 00:04:09.640
And you know how the men next to you, how you yourself act when you're asleep.

00:04:10.600 --> 00:04:16.540
These fellows, like you, are behaving as if the enemy were right down the corridor, or outside the window.

00:04:17.280 --> 00:04:20.500
They're remembering, always remembering.

00:04:23.440 --> 00:04:27.400
This is patient Corporal Ben Edwards, United States Marine Corps.

00:04:28.040 --> 00:04:33.120
If we find out Edwards' trouble, it will help us to understand what's happening to these other men.

00:04:33.720 --> 00:04:38.600
And what's more to the point, it will help us find out what the trouble is with you.

00:04:40.960 --> 00:04:47.940
As you can see, Edwards is having a rough time of it, and you don't have a rough time like this without there being a cause for it.

00:04:49.560 --> 00:04:53.620
To discover that cause, we'll have to see what he's dreaming about.

00:04:56.580 --> 00:05:02.100
[Edwards:] Keep five paces. Keep five paces.

00:05:04.800 --> 00:05:10.400
Keep five paces. Keep five paces.

00:05:11.720 --> 00:05:16.900
Keep five paces.

00:05:19.460 --> 00:05:37.480
[Soldiers make their way through jungle stealthily.]

00:05:38.200 --> 00:05:39.340
[Edwards jumps out of his sleep.]

00:05:46.700 --> 00:05:49.380
[Narrator:] Well, that's why Edwards is tired each morning.

00:05:50.240 --> 00:05:54.380
Possibly it's the same sort of thing that makes you feel all wrung out these mornings too.

00:05:55.600 --> 00:05:59.800
You can see that he's remembering some battle he was in, or at least parts of it,

00:06:00.560 --> 00:06:06.920
and that his remembering has no relationship to where he is, in the safe hospital far away from the enemy.

00:06:08.320 --> 00:06:10.860
So now let's go back to the beginning and see how this came about.

00:06:12.400 --> 00:06:27.300
[ Music ]

00:06:28.020 --> 00:06:40.700
[The men sing together in their bunks.]

00:06:41.000 --> 00:06:46.240
[Soldier 1:] So I said to her, "For cripes' sake, you've had everything I got, and now you want a cup of coffee."

00:06:46.440 --> 00:06:48.080
[Soldier 2:] Gee, what a lousy babe.

00:06:48.600 --> 00:06:50.940
[Soldier 3:] Wonder how much longer before we get there.

00:06:51.380 --> 00:06:52.913
[Soldier 4:] We're seven days out.

00:06:53.420 --> 00:06:55.340
[Soldier 5:] Seven days is a hell of a long time.

00:06:55.340 --> 00:06:56.680
[Soldier 6:] I know.

00:06:57.820 --> 00:07:22.320
[One soldier plays a harmonica and others sing along.]

00:07:22.420 --> 00:07:24.180
[Soldier 7:] Jeez, do you have to go all the time?

00:07:25.400 --> 00:07:29.260
[Soldier 8:] Well ain't there some law about wetting a uniform, government property?

00:07:30.140 --> 00:07:33.240
[Soldier 9:] Wouldn't be the war, would it? Nothing like that.

00:07:34.140 --> 00:07:36.140
[Soldier 8:] No, nothing like that.

00:07:36.380 --> 00:07:40.613
[Soldier 7:] Well, it won't be long anyway. Maybe tomorrow. Then you can make fly whenever you want to.

00:07:40.613 --> 00:07:42.679
You'll know what a battle's all about.

00:07:42.680 --> 00:07:44.813
[Soldier 8:] You think it'll be tomorrow?

00:07:44.813 --> 00:07:49.213
[Soldier 7:] I hope so...kinda keyed up being a sailor.

00:07:49.220 --> 00:07:50.260
[Soldier 9:] It sure does.

00:07:51.660 --> 00:07:54.480
[Soldier 8:] We must be a thousand miles north of Tulagi.

00:07:55.120 --> 00:07:56.600
[Soldier 7:] Yeah, or west.

00:07:57.800 --> 00:08:00.600
[Soldier 8:] Jeez, for a buddy, you sure can contradict a guy.

00:08:01.000 --> 00:08:02.500
[Soldier 7:] Well, that's what a buddy is for.

00:08:02.520 --> 00:08:03.660
[Soldier 8:] Yeah.

00:08:04.380 --> 00:08:06.300
[Soldier 7:] Oh, is that what you're going to do with the Japs?

00:08:06.780 --> 00:08:08.300
[Soldier 8:] Yeah, and this and this.

00:08:08.320 --> 00:08:09.780
[Soldier 7:] Oh, wait a minute.

00:08:09.780 --> 00:08:14.513
[Sounds of guys wrestling on the floor.]

00:08:14.520 --> 00:08:17.240
[Soldier 1:] Kids, that's all. Wait 'til they get there.

00:08:18.146 --> 00:08:22.080
[Soldier 2:] Yeah, seven days. It will be soon now.

00:08:24.340 --> 00:08:26.580
[Narrator:] Well, that's where it began.

00:08:27.300 --> 00:08:30.013
You can see that Corporal Edwards is excited.

00:08:30.300 --> 00:08:34.240
Maybe the chap with the dry pipe is right. Maybe he is behaving like a kid.

00:08:34.860 --> 00:08:40.340
But that's because he and his buddy, Hal, are tense, and all the other men with them, each in his own way,

00:08:41.560 --> 00:08:45.340
they're beginning to be afraid, dreading what will happen in battle,

00:08:45.680 --> 00:08:49.340
wondering whether they will master their fear or whether it will defeat them.

00:08:50.500 --> 00:08:56.180
And the answer to that they don't find out until the seven days have become eight or nine or two weeks

00:08:56.180 --> 00:08:59.280
and they are wherever it is the enemy is.

00:08:59.340 --> 00:09:22.040
[Explosions]

00:09:22.400 --> 00:09:27.320
[Edwards:] Careful of those GI pants. [Hal:] It looks like like get or get got, all right.

00:09:30.620 --> 00:09:33.200
[Narrator:] That's it. They're like the cat.

00:09:33.640 --> 00:09:37.460
They're seeing their enemy, and they're scared, but they're not yellow.

00:09:39.420 --> 00:09:44.040
All those symptoms he had were to get him ready for this, to make him a good fighting man.

00:09:45.260 --> 00:09:51.613
He's using his fear, just as you did at first, to make him keen, aware of every danger about him,

00:09:51.620 --> 00:09:53.040
so that he can meet it.

00:09:55.040 --> 00:09:58.320
Sure, he's afraid, and so is every other man.

00:09:59.220 --> 00:10:06.340
But see how his fear helps him, see it snap that rifle into firing position, see it carry him forward, pursuing the enemy,

00:10:07.220 --> 00:10:09.300
see it give him almost second sight.

00:10:10.400 --> 00:10:11.940
Edwards is a good corporal.

00:10:12.320 --> 00:10:15.800
He's really fighting because his fear has been useful to him.

00:10:16.640 --> 00:10:24.700
[Gunfire]

00:10:25.640 --> 00:10:26.740
[Commander:] Take it easy.

00:10:28.720 --> 00:10:29.640
[Hal:] Where are they?

00:10:31.900 --> 00:10:33.800
[Officer:] Block it off. They're on the run.

00:10:34.120 --> 00:10:36.320
Dig in here. Make this your position and hold it.

00:10:36.480 --> 00:10:37.080
[Stone:] Aye aye, sir.

00:10:37.140 --> 00:10:37.700
[Officer:] Over here, men.

00:10:40.220 --> 00:10:42.040
How is it out there?

00:10:42.047 --> 00:10:43.180
[Bryan:] It's pretty quiet.

00:10:43.180 --> 00:10:46.320
[Edwards:] Okay Bryan, you and Stone go on ahead for security.

00:10:49.880 --> 00:10:51.600
All right, fellows, shovel it deep.

00:10:51.980 --> 00:10:55.300
Chubby, over there on that scrub for you, there's a good spot.

00:10:55.540 --> 00:10:57.140
Lawrence, over there by that tree.

00:10:58.660 --> 00:11:00.140
Hal, you and I will dig here.

00:11:00.860 --> 00:11:19.880
[The men dig foxholes.]

00:11:20.240 --> 00:11:22.360
[Hal:] Still, I like apple pie better.

00:11:22.780 --> 00:11:26.900
[Edwards:] Oh, I don't know. My money, I'll take that blonde in Frisco.

00:11:27.440 --> 00:11:31.420
[Hal] You mean the one with hair on her legs?[Edwards:] Yeah, blonde hair.

00:11:32.500 --> 00:11:36.320
[Sounds of shelling.]

00:11:38.440 --> 00:11:40.820
[Hal:] I said I liked apple pie better.

00:11:43.980 --> 00:11:46.500
[Edwards:] The bastards don't know where we are. Come on, let's get going.

00:11:56.860 --> 00:11:59.780
[Narrator:] You see? The immediate danger has been removed.

00:12:00.140 --> 00:12:02.180
At least it's not so bad as it was.

00:12:02.520 --> 00:12:05.420
And so they joke, and are, again, not afraid.

00:12:06.260 --> 00:12:08.740
Their fear was useful to them in the battle.

00:12:09.180 --> 00:12:12.060
Now they don't need it, and so it disappears.

00:12:12.900 --> 00:12:16.580
If it weren't to disappear now, then the men would not be well.

00:12:17.300 --> 00:12:19.580
But like the cat, they have lost their symptoms.

00:12:20.280 --> 00:12:24.060
Their dog is off in the jungle, and their fear has gone with him.

00:12:25.120 --> 00:12:29.960
Their fear has served its purpose, making them angry, putting them in condition red.

00:12:31.260 --> 00:12:34.080
It's been a beneficial tool in the time of crisis.

00:12:34.340 --> 00:12:36.200
It appeared when it was needed.

00:12:36.980 --> 00:12:43.540
But don't forget, however, Corporal Edwards remembers everything that has happened. Now he knows what fear is like.

00:12:44.420 --> 00:12:47.220
From this time on, his knowledge is always with him.

00:12:48.440 --> 00:12:49.740
Let's see where it leads him.

00:12:50.820 --> 00:12:56.647
[Men hike through jungle, rain falling and leaves and twigs crackling]

00:12:56.647 --> 00:12:58.760
[Soldier:] If we could only be sure the sons of bitches weren't out there.

00:12:59.920 --> 00:13:02.113
[Soldier 2:] My own mother told me there'd be days like this.

00:13:02.113 --> 00:13:04.146
[Soldier 3:] Yeah? Why didn't you shoot her?

00:13:04.147 --> 00:13:07.080
[Edwards]  All right, fellows, knock it off. Making too much noise.

00:13:07.080 --> 00:13:10.760
[Stone:] Sure. [Edwards:] It would make things a hell of a lot easier if you could sing.

00:13:13.500 --> 00:13:23.313
[ Sounds of splashing and talking]

00:13:23.313 --> 00:13:25.213
[Stone:] Somebody ought to teach them marksmanship.

00:13:25.220 --> 00:13:26.860
[Hal:] Yeah, their chests must look mighty bare.

00:13:27.880 --> 00:13:30.680
[Bryan:] Wonder what they're doing with all that tin they got in Singapore.

00:13:34.940 --> 00:13:37.780
[Officer:] I don't know why you guys are always scrubbing clothes.

00:13:37.780 --> 00:13:41.600
With all those shots, it really makes a hell of a good washing machine.

00:13:41.880 --> 00:13:43.520
[Shells splash in the river they're bathing in.]

00:13:53.940 --> 00:14:00.120
[Edwards:] Christ, my feet fell asleep. Cooped up like this.

00:14:05.100 --> 00:14:05.980
Where are they?

00:14:07.220 --> 00:14:11.140
[Hal:] I don't think they're there. It's a week now since we heard 'em.

00:14:11.500 --> 00:14:15.280
[Edwards:] No, it isn't. Eight days and five hours.

00:14:16.940 --> 00:14:17.860
[Hal:] Okay.

00:14:20.620 --> 00:14:22.720
[Edwards] Gee, who woulda thought it'd been like this?

00:14:24.740 --> 00:14:26.900
Four weeks in this goddamned hole.

00:14:27.700 --> 00:14:30.580
Four weeks of lying on your belly and feeding the mosquitoes.

00:14:31.440 --> 00:14:38.940
[Hal:] Yeah. It's getting so I'm forgetting everything else, including what a man lives for.

00:14:40.620 --> 00:14:41.760
[Edwards:]  Even Sarah Ann?

00:14:42.520 --> 00:14:46.280
[Hal:] Including Sarah Ann. And that's some crock.

00:14:47.320 --> 00:14:53.480
[A soldier crawls on his belly through the brush toward the two men.]

00:14:53.480 --> 00:14:54.913
[Stone:] There isn't a goddamn Jap out there.

00:14:54.920 --> 00:14:59.000
So why in hell don't we do something about it? Why don't we get a move on? Why don't we take over?

00:15:00.120 --> 00:15:01.560
See, not a goddam...!

00:15:01.700 --> 00:15:17.780
[A shell explodes and the soldier who crawled up is silent.]

00:15:17.780 --> 00:15:19.980
[Edwards:] Come on, let's get him out of here.

00:15:27.180 --> 00:15:29.780
[Hal] Now, to cook food, all you have to do is put it in the sun.

00:15:29.780 --> 00:15:34.013
[Edwards:] Yeah, if the sun don't get it, the maggots will. [Hal:] In any case, brother, you'll eat it.

00:15:34.013 --> 00:15:35.646
[Edwards:] And like it.

00:15:35.647 --> 00:15:37.347
[Hal:] K rations, X rations.

00:15:37.347 --> 00:15:46.020
[An alarm sounds and the men react.]

00:15:48.280 --> 00:16:01.460
[The soldiers scramble as enemies begin shooting at their camp.]

00:16:01.460 --> 00:16:07.280
[Edwards] Every day. Every damn day. When are they gonna let us eat in peace?

00:16:12.140 --> 00:16:40.580
[The soldiers wait silently in the jungle in their assigned positions.]

00:16:40.580 --> 00:17:16.860
[A shrill sound, the click of a gun, and then shouting in Japanese.]

00:17:23.700 --> 00:17:28.400
[Narrator:] We've only shown you part of all that happened, just little incidents through the weeks.

00:17:29.260 --> 00:17:32.280
But I guess you can see where Edwards is going and why.

00:17:33.200 --> 00:17:35.280
He's been kept always on the alert.

00:17:35.680 --> 00:17:42.860
Those fear responses haven't had much chance to relax because the enemy, the danger, is always present.

00:17:44.120 --> 00:17:49.800
It's just as if the dog never went away from the cat, left the cat always like this.

00:17:50.800 --> 00:17:55.940
Naturally, you can't freeze forever in a position like this. Something is bound to give.

00:17:56.760 --> 00:18:01.660
But usually such events as you have seen are not enough to cause combat fatigue.

00:18:02.280 --> 00:18:04.000
Some climax has to happen.

00:18:04.620 --> 00:18:06.540
And with Edwards, this is what it was.

00:18:07.920 --> 00:18:13.960
Oh, and by the way, this is the last of our really noisy scenes, so please bear with us.

00:18:16.180 --> 00:18:20.160
[Officer:] You already know what we're going to do. Anybody have any questions before we go?

00:18:21.620 --> 00:18:25.120
All right, when we leave here, you men with a machine gun, go back and join your outfit.

00:18:25.920 --> 00:18:28.700
We're going to clean out the Japs between here and the river.

00:18:28.720 --> 00:18:34.940
Our patrol will be the right assault platoon, and we'll move out of here and over towards the assembly area.

00:18:35.820 --> 00:18:38.180
When we move out, keep at least five paces.

00:18:38.980 --> 00:18:40.500
There might be some snipers.

00:18:42.460 --> 00:18:44.420
You all have your rations and ammunition.

00:18:46.240 --> 00:18:50.160
All right, sergeant, we'll move out immediately down this trail.

00:18:50.480 --> 00:18:52.720
I'll meet you at the front. All right, let's go.

00:18:52.720 --> 00:18:54.540
[Sergeant:] Okay, men, let's go.

00:18:58.820 --> 00:19:25.980
[The men walk carefully along the trail.]

00:19:26.360 --> 00:19:39.320
[Edwards, whispering:] Five paces. Five paces.
Keep five paces. Keep five paces.

00:19:59.980 --> 00:20:06.240
[The soldiers pace through the trees toward their next position.]

00:20:06.780 --> 00:20:08.080
[A shell explodes.]

00:20:12.380 --> 00:20:13.320
[Sergeant:] Spread out.

00:20:19.420 --> 00:20:30.140
[The men continue through the jungle.]

00:20:33.100 --> 00:20:35.440
[A shell explodes next to one soldier, who falls to the ground.]

00:21:07.720 --> 00:21:09.320
[Edwards:] It could have been me.

00:21:26.320 --> 00:21:28.100
[More shells explode.]

00:21:37.760 --> 00:21:56.160
[A barrage of gunfire begins.]

00:21:57.040 --> 00:21:59.620
[Narrator:] Well, that's it.

00:22:01.640 --> 00:22:04.860
Now we know what Corporal Edwards dreams about every night in the hospital.

00:22:05.940 --> 00:22:10.640
But why? Because he'd had as much as he could take.

00:22:11.880 --> 00:22:17.420
He'd been too full of fear too long. Not that he's a coward. You've seen that he isn't.

00:22:18.420 --> 00:22:24.680
But there's a point in every man, in every man, beyond which he cannot go.

00:22:25.280 --> 00:22:28.100
That point with Edwards was reached when his buddy was killed.

00:22:28.620 --> 00:22:32.320
It could have been something else. It just happened with Edwards, that's what it was.

00:22:33.940 --> 00:22:37.460
From now on, you'll see that Edwards no longer behaves like himself.

00:22:38.420 --> 00:22:44.580
Everything he does or says is a symptom of illness. As his friends put it, it's got him.

00:22:46.180 --> 00:22:54.213
But if we interpret these symptoms, we'll see that what looks like mysterious or strange behavior isn't so illogical after all,

00:22:54.660 --> 00:22:56.360
but has meaning and purpose.

00:22:57.660 --> 00:23:03.940
It's just that Edwards won't accept fear, won't admit that he's reached his breaking point.

00:23:10.640 --> 00:23:12.100
[Edwards:] Hand me that gizmo there.

00:23:14.140 --> 00:23:27.000
[The guns clink as he tries to clean out the chamber.]

00:23:27.013 --> 00:23:30.746
This goddamn thing won't work, never did work and never will work.

00:23:30.747 --> 00:23:32.647
[Hal:] Don't try to rush it. Sure it'll work.

00:23:32.647 --> 00:23:34.200
[Edwards:] I said it wouldn't, didn't I?

00:23:34.980 --> 00:23:37.840
I don't think the gear we've got in this gook place is any good anyway.

00:23:37.847 --> 00:23:39.847
You'd think they'd send us the best.

00:23:39.847 --> 00:23:42.713
[Hal:] What's in your pants, red ants, or did you get hit?

00:23:42.713 --> 00:23:44.846
[Edwards:] Well, we're the ones who are fighting this war.

00:23:44.846 --> 00:23:46.520
You think they'd give us something to fight it with.

00:23:49.880 --> 00:23:57.600
[Man singing and whistling: They sent for the army to come to Tulagi but General MacArthur said no...

00:23:58.400 --> 00:24:06.340
and this is the reason it isn't the season, besides there ain't no USO...]

00:24:12.040 --> 00:24:13.400
[Edwards:] For Christ's sake.

00:24:17.020 --> 00:24:29.200
[Low cricket and bird sounds as the soldiers try to sleep and begin to dream.]

00:25:00.960 --> 00:25:12.480
[Edwards kneels, jumps up and aims his rifle, then leans against a tree, looking tense.]

00:25:30.040 --> 00:25:35.740
[Edwards fires his rifle into the trees repeatedly.]

00:25:38.980 --> 00:25:40.840
[Sergeant:] What the hell is the matter?

00:25:40.840 --> 00:25:43.380
[Edwards:] Over there behind that tree, there was a Jap, sir. I saw him, so I shot him.

00:25:49.260 --> 00:25:51.160
[Captain:] The fact remains, Edwards, that you fired.

00:25:52.980 --> 00:25:56.840
You knew the orders, you knew it meant the brig if you shot at random.

00:25:57.800 --> 00:25:58.820
[Edwards:] Yes, sir.

00:25:59.960 --> 00:26:00.640
[Captain:] Why did you do it?

00:26:01.840 --> 00:26:06.680
[Edwards:] There was a Jap there, sir. A twig snapped and I saw something move.
I saw him very clearly. It was a Jap.

00:26:08.540 --> 00:26:09.900
[Captain:] Did you kill him?

00:26:10.200 --> 00:26:13.340
[Edwards:] Behind the tree, something moved. I saw him and I shot him.

00:26:14.120 --> 00:26:15.540
[Captain:] Did anybody find the body?

00:26:16.640 --> 00:26:17.980
[Edwards:] I shot him.

00:26:19.040 --> 00:26:19.740
[Captain:] I see.

00:26:24.440 --> 00:26:26.660
Not feeling very well, are you, Corporal?

00:26:28.860 --> 00:26:30.340
[Edwards:] No, sir.

00:26:31.200 --> 00:26:32.640
[Captain:] How is your appetite?

00:26:32.640 --> 00:26:36.300
[Edwards:] Oh, it's lousy. What I do eat won't stick. It's the slum we get here.

00:26:38.300 --> 00:26:39.160
[Captain:] Are you sure of that?

00:26:40.500 --> 00:26:41.820
[Edwards:] Well, I vomit, don't I?

00:26:43.620 --> 00:26:45.200
[Captain:] Yes, I guess you do.

00:26:57.580 --> 00:26:59.680
[Narrator:] Well, there's the story.

00:27:00.540 --> 00:27:07.980
Perhaps you recognize the symptoms: nerves and jumpiness, hostility, insomnia, vomiting.

00:27:08.920 --> 00:27:13.080
Probably he'd tell you he had headaches if you asked him, or diarrhea.

00:27:13.880 --> 00:27:15.800
He's lost his sense of proportion too.

00:27:16.860 --> 00:27:21.840
He knows deep inside himself that there was no Jap behind those leaves, but he won't admit it,

00:27:21.840 --> 00:27:22.860
not even to himself.

00:27:24.520 --> 00:27:26.180
Now, how does all this come about?

00:27:27.160 --> 00:27:31.860
Well, you remember our cat, and that the cat had all these physical symptoms of fear?

00:27:33.000 --> 00:27:36.220
And you remember that Edwards had those same symptoms?

00:27:36.360 --> 00:27:39.940
The blood rushed from his stomach, his heart pounded, his hands were sweaty.

00:27:40.820 --> 00:27:44.980
And that didn't happen once, but many times over and over again.

00:27:46.180 --> 00:27:53.240
And the load kept getting bigger and bigger, always increasing until it was just too big a load to carry.

00:27:53.880 --> 00:27:59.620
And since he remembered all the times, he got so he had the symptoms even when there was no cause.

00:28:00.520 --> 00:28:02.480
That's why he couldn't hold his food any longer.

00:28:03.660 --> 00:28:06.480
His stomach was physically constricted with his fear.

00:28:06.880 --> 00:28:07.940
And so he vomited.

00:28:09.180 --> 00:28:12.940
You know that men going into battle often vomit or soil their pants.

00:28:13.920 --> 00:28:20.900
Well, men who carry their fear with them after the battle go on doing the same things because their bodies are always in condition red.

00:28:22.120 --> 00:28:26.480
And that's why Edwards is here in the hospital, because he can't forget.

00:28:28.120 --> 00:28:30.780
He always acts as if there were an enemy present.

00:28:32.020 --> 00:28:37.560
On a peaceful street, he behaves as if there were a Jap ready to jump from behind any parked car.

00:28:40.000 --> 00:28:46.120
If somebody drops something near him, he thinks a mortar's got his number, or that he's being dive-bombed.

00:28:48.120 --> 00:28:54.780
Maybe instead of getting it out, you've got it inside, and it hangs in your chest like a lump of lead.

00:28:55.640 --> 00:29:00.020
Or maybe you've thought about it so much that it makes your head swim and you're dizzy.

00:29:01.200 --> 00:29:05.920
Or maybe you're like the patient I saw the other day who took it out on his pals and his wife,

00:29:05.920 --> 00:29:10.000
and acted as if they were the Japs and the Jerries and was edgy and irritable.

00:29:11.500 --> 00:29:16.713
That's fear, if you call it by its right name, and don't blame your stomach or your eyes

00:29:16.720 --> 00:29:20.800
or your bowels for acting the way nature intended them to act when you're scared.

00:29:22.520 --> 00:29:28.240
Yes, it sounds simple, but the cure is far from easy.

00:29:29.380 --> 00:29:31.260
That's why your doctor sees you so often.

00:29:35.700 --> 00:29:43.380
That's why you have to do physical work, go to discussion groups, play games, learn about the happenings of the world.

00:29:44.940 --> 00:29:49.440
You're getting well because you're learning what fear is and how to handle it.

00:29:50.620 --> 00:29:55.100
And when you get your problem worked out, you will find you don't fight against fear,

00:29:55.500 --> 00:30:00.200
you just get it lined up so that it works to your best interest when you're face-to-face with the enemy.

00:30:02.160 --> 00:30:08.780
As soon as you've learned that the bullets shot at you yesterday or last month or last year can't hurt you today,

00:30:09.360 --> 00:30:11.200
you've learned the first big lesson.

00:30:12.220 --> 00:30:17.060
Then and only then, you get into condition red at the right time and in the right place.

00:30:18.120 --> 00:30:23.960
You accept fear as part of living, something to be taken as a help, not as a handicap.

00:30:25.140 --> 00:30:30.780
You and Edwards and the other boys, one fine day, will be as fit as a new silver dollar.

00:30:31.740 --> 00:30:39.380
And you will be shoving off well, not only better, but better than ever because you've learned something

00:30:39.380 --> 00:30:41.020
that can only be learned the hard way,

00:30:41.940 --> 00:30:48.760
that fear is a fighting man's friend, if he learns how to run it and not be run by it.

00:30:51.380 --> 00:31:00.340
[Music]

